# Panchayat Bill
Tamil title: ஊராட்சி மன்றச் சட்ட முன்வடிவு

The statement, "We will give you as much as you collect in house tax," looks very attractive on paper. The document says that a village panchayat with a population of one thousand will receive Rs. 1,250. But when one calculates the number of houses in a village and then visits it, one sees that while two new houses are being built, twenty old houses have collapsed. People do not have the money even to rebuild those old, ruined houses. Perhaps two new houses are built by people who have earned money in the city and wish to display their earnings to the villagers. I therefore wish to submit humbly that if we expect house tax to yield sufficient revenue in the villages, we will certainly be disappointed.

Moreover, since it is claimed that many benefits will result from entrusting the Community Development Scheme to these bodies, we must examine briefly the Community Project connected with this bill. The view has spread widely that the Community Project has thus far served as a publicity booth for the ruling party. The Finance Minister may either refute this with statistics or provoke me by reciting my ancestry. Whichever method he employs, people across the country broadly regard the Community Project as a publicity booth for the ruling party.

Merely transferring it to the village will not automatically benefit the country. Recognising this, we must examine, as the Finance Minister said at the outset, the relationship between village administration and village amenities. Good administrative schemes alone will not create amenities for a village, and administration cannot function where no amenities exist.

A system of administration can be prepared and given to the village, but responsibility for creating amenities lies outside this bill. Not only does it lie outside the bill, poverty in the villages has grown to such a degree that no party in this august House can ignore it.

If we now consider the means of increasing the amenities available to village people, it becomes possible to calculate: an increase of so much in amenities permits so much tax to be collected; collecting so much tax yields so much matching grant; that matching grant produces so much total revenue; and that revenue raises their standard of living.

Yet village people have been given the responsibility of increasing their own amenities, after merely asking whether there is any means of doing so in the countryside. I understand the Finance Minister's view to be that democracy means distributing responsibilities. But the responsibility to provide a scheme that people can bear belongs to those who govern.

Even if people are weak in body and spirit, when they are given responsibility and facilities, they possess the ability to discharge that responsibility. Do not assume that the people of the villages lack ability merely because they have not been given the facilities needed to perform these tasks. They will fulfil every responsibility they accept. Many among them may be future Finance Ministers, and many may be future Chief Ministers.

As the Finance Minister said, we would not refuse to make someone a Minister merely because that person was uneducated. Democracy would not permit it. Even if village people have not passed through ordinary schooling, they can look at the sky and tell when it will rain, look at a crop and tell when it can be harvested, and look at the manure and tell how many *kalams* it will yield. The training they have acquired through practical reason can be put to use in bodies such as these.

Such village people have entered even this House. One may say, if one wishes, that they do not speak with the same legal subtlety as we do, but no one can regard them as inferior. I strongly urge that the good sense found in the villages be put to use for the villages. If this bill provides room for that, incorporate it.

The Finance Minister said, "If you propose only amendments that can be adopted, I shall accept them." I think his reason for saying so is this: "Where are these people going to propose amendments fit to be adopted? They will say something superficial; if I cleverly offer three or four arguments in reply, that will settle it." He places his faith in his cleverness, not in his heart.

**C. Subramaniam:** I have faith in your cleverness. I do not believe that you suffer from any lack of it. [Laughter.]

**C. N. Annadurai:** We may both belong to the same kind. [Laughter.] I ask that you show greater concern in your heart and introduce into this law measures that will enable the village people to live. I appreciate the rulers' intention. I appreciate their showing the village people this degree of love and concern. I shall conclude by expressing my present doubt as to whether this concern has perhaps arisen because those people are voters too.

---NOTES---
`Kalam` is retained as the Tamil measure named in the source; its exact capacity varied historically by place and commodity.
